import * as T from '../vendor/three.module.js';
// Authored ramps; reconstruction-inspired multiply/add, no Sega data or shader copy.
// SA1 / Dreamcast-style light, evaluated per vertex like baked vertex lighting (gouraud, so towers, roofs, domes and
// crowns carry soft interpolated highlights). The ramp index comes from a baked key: tops take the sun at full height
// plus a sky share, vertical faces take the sun at a reduced, glancing share (so tops are the brightest value at any
// sun azimuth), and a small camera-relative term (from the view's left, slightly behind the walls facing the camera)
// keeps the two visible wall orientations apart at every Q/E yaw, even with the sun straight behind or ahead.
// The ramp's shade end is its ambient part and the rest is the sun. Tops see the open sky: the ramp's shade colour and
// the sky fill at full strength. Walls and undersides see less sky and more of the sunlit ground: the shade colour with
// its chroma trimmed per sky preset (Day: stone stays warm in shade instead of periwinkle), a quarter of the sky fill
// and a warm ground bounce; the hemisphere tint (sky side / ground) still applies. A narrow rim lights only faces
// seen almost edge-on (curved silhouettes against the sky), never whole wall faces.
// The sliders keep their meaning: gain = brightness, contrast = ramp crispness (0 = soft half-Lambert wrap, 1 = gouraud
// Lambert), relief = hemisphere/fill/bounce strength, ramp ends = warmth/shade or the sky preset's pair.
